  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1200 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A reinforcing cold front will move through the \nregion this evening, bringing a period of scattered showers to \nportions of central NC.\n\nA reinforcing cold front will move through central NC this evening. \nThe front is presently draped from central VA into western NC. The \nfront will approach early this evening, moving through late tonight, \nbringing the true cold air to the region that will linger through \nthe weekend and early next week.\n\nAlong and ahead of the front, models are in pretty good agreement \nthat a compact shortwave presently over KY/TN will round the base of \nthe trough and move through our region this evening. Height falls \nrange from 30 to 70-dm, providing notable forcing aloft. Forecast \nsoundings from the RAP/GFS/RRFS also indicate some weak instability \nand saturation at the dendritic growth zone region, indicative of \nmixed-phase precipitation production aloft. Given these \nconsiderations and some possible low-level convergence along the \nfront, we should see cloud cover markedly increase late this \nafternoon and evening, with a blossoming of precipitation from the \neastern Piedmont and Triangle into the northeast Piedmont and \ncentral to northern Coastal Plain. The 12z HRRR/RRFS have come in \nhigher with precipitation chances, along with rain amounts, with a \nstripe of a tenth of an inch or more over portions of the area from \nthe Triangle north and east. If we can we a few lightning strikes \nlike we saw yesterday, would not be surprised if a few reports of \ngraupel are reported due to the low freezing levels. Some gusts to \n20-25 mph could also occur with any convectively-enhanced radar \nechoes, feeding on the dry sub-cloud layer inverted-V profiles seen \nin soundings.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Frost/Freeze headlines remain in place tonight, \nwith a Freeze Warning for all of central NC Saturday night.\n\nFrost/Freeze headlines have remained unchanged tonight into early \nSaturday. While the aforementioned shortwave and cold front moving \nin this evening will increase cloud cover, we still expect clouds to \nweaken and disperse after midnight and towards the early morning \nhours of Saturday. Some light stirring may also inhibit lows from \ngetting below freezing in the Warning area, but given little change \nin guidance with respect to lows, decided to keep frost/freeze \nheadlines intact.\n\nA more widespread freeze is expected Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning. A Continental Polar high of around 1030-mb will settle \nacross the Mid MS valley region. Although not in the most ideal \nposition, the pressure gradient should relax, allowing winds to go \ncalm. Coupled with low-level thicknesses of 1304-1310m, should \nsupport a widespread freeze of upper 20s to low 30s, coldest across \nthe Piedmont. Given the consistent signal in guidance, decided to \nupgrade the Freeze Watch to a Warning for Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning.",
